 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Performance:** The method introduces latency due to multiple render passes (cycling through peaks) and the overhead of `gl.readPixels` and WASM inference [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Hardware:** Requires a GPU capable of WebGL rendering and a CPU capable of running WebAssembly [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Strengths:** Eliminates manual tuning for fluorescence microscopy data; integrates directly into web-based viewers [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Limitations:** Specific to the tent-shaped transfer function described; requires the ONNX Runtime Web peer dependency; sensitive to CDN availability for WASM loading [S1, unofficial].
-*   **When to use:** When browsing large collections of volume data with varying intensity distributions where manual peak adjustment is prohibitive [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Time Complexity:** Depends on the inference time of MobileNetV3-Small on WebAssembly and the cost of `gl.readPixels` and tensor construction. Not given in the sources.
+*   **Memory Complexity:** Requires storing the ONNX model in memory and temporary buffers for rendering and tensor construction. Not given in the sources.
+*   **Performance:** Designed for web-based volume rendering (Three.js). Performance is constrained by WebAssembly overhead and WebGL readback latency. Not given in the sources.
+*   **Strengths:** Automates a tedious manual process; lightweight model suitable for web environments; integrates directly with React hooks.
+*   **Limitations:** Requires a CDN connection for WASM loading; dependent on the quality of the training data for the MobileNetV3 model; limited to the specific transfer function shape described (tent-shaped alpha).
 
 ## Background
-The method is implemented in the `web-vol-viewer` project by Janelia Science Computing [S1, unofficial]. It is designed for fluorescence microscopy data, building on the transfer function principles described in Wan et al., "FluoRender: An Application of 2D Image Space Methods for 3D and 4D Confocal Microscopy Data Visualization in Neurobiology Research" (2012) [S1, unofficial]. The desktop application VVD Viewer also utilizes this transfer function [S1, unofficial].
+This method is implemented in the `web-vol-viewer` project by JaneliaSciComp [S1, unofficial]. It is designed for fluorescence microscopy data visualization, building on the principles of the FluoRender application described by Wan et al. (2012) [S1, unofficial]. The approach leverages the availability of ONNX Runtime Web to enable machine learning inference in the browser.
 
